When P2 is complete and committed, run `baton done P2 --notes "<what later phases need to know>"` and end your turn. Gates that take more than about 2 minutes, or need state of their own, go to baton: `baton verify "<command>" --notes "<where the phase stands>"` runs it on HEAD (commit first) in a lane of its own while you keep working on what does not depend on it, and baton gives you the result when it ends; `baton await` does the same for something outside the project, such as a CI run. Quick checks run in place. Once the phase is complete and committed, run `baton done` even while its gate still runs: the phase is done when the gate passes, and reopens if it fails. If something goes wrong, keep moving on a reversible path and tell the human: `baton note` (you took it), `baton propose` (a reversible way around a problem; the human may veto it by a deadline), `baton blocked --tried` (only if every way forward is irreversible). Never type /compact yourself.